    {
     "n": "9b",
     "answer": "<p>E<sub>n</sub> = n<sup>2</sup>h<sup>2</sup>/8mL<sup>2</sup>.</p><div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">(i) E<sub>1</sub> = (6.626 × 10<sup>−34</sup>)<sup>2</sup>/(8 × 0.2 × (0.02)<sup>2</sup>) = 6.86 × 10<sup>−64</sup> J</div><div class=\"eq\">E<sub>2</sub> = 4E<sub>1</sub> = 2.74 × 10<sup>−63</sup> J, E<sub>3</sub> = 9E<sub>1</sub> = 6.17 × 10<sup>−63</sup> J</div><div class=\"eq\">(ii) E<sub>1</sub> = (6.626 × 10<sup>−34</sup>)<sup>2</sup>/(8 × 10<sup>−31</sup> × (5 × 10<sup>−9</sup>)<sup>2</sup>) = 2.20 × 10<sup>−20</sup> J = 0.137 eV</div><div class=\"eq\">E<sub>2</sub> = 8.78 × 10<sup>−20</sup> J (0.549 eV), E<sub>3</sub> = 1.98 × 10<sup>−19</sup> J (1.23 eV)</div></div><div class=\"result\"><p>Marble: levels about 10<sup>−63</sup> J apart, effectively continuous; light particle in 5 nm: levels of order 0.1 to 1 eV, clearly quantised</p></div></div><p>E ∝ 1/mL<sup>2</sup>: quantisation is observable only for very small masses in very small regions. (The exponent of the reduced mass is hard to read on the scan; 10<sup>−31</sup> kg, close to the electron mass, is used here.)</p>"
    },
    {
     "n": "10a",
     "answer": "<p><b>Condition.</b> |Ψ⟩ = a|00⟩ + b|01⟩ + c|10⟩ + d|11⟩ is a product state only if it equals (α|0⟩ + β|1⟩) ⊗ (γ|0⟩ + δ|1⟩), which gives a = αγ, b = αδ, c = βγ, d = βδ and hence ad = bc. So the state is <b>entangled if ad − bc ≠ 0</b> (it cannot be factorised).</p><p><b>Maximal entanglement.</b> |ad − bc| ≤ ½ for normalised states; it is maximally entangled when |ad − bc| = ½, each qubit alone is completely random (probability ½ each) and the outcomes are perfectly correlated in every basis. The four possibilities are the Bell states |Φ<sup>±</sup>⟩ = (|00⟩ ± |11⟩)/√2 and |Ψ<sup>±</sup>⟩ = (|01⟩ ± |10⟩)/√2.</p><p><b>Laboratory realisations.</b></p><ul><li>Spontaneous parametric down-conversion: a laser photon in a nonlinear (BBO) crystal splits into two photons with entangled polarisations, e.g. (|HV⟩ + |VH⟩)/√2.</li><li>Quantum gates: a Hadamard followed by a CNOT on |00⟩ gives |Φ<sup>+</sup>⟩ in trapped-ion or superconducting qubits, driven by laser or microwave pulses.</li></ul><p><b>Applications.</b></p><ul><li>Quantum teleportation: a shared Bell pair plus two classical bits transfers an unknown qubit state.</li><li>Quantum key distribution (E91): correlations of entangled pairs give a secret key, and eavesdropping disturbs them.</li><li>Also superdense coding, quantum computing speed-up and precision sensing.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
     "n": "10b",
     "answer": "<p><b>(i)</b> After removing the global phase and normalising, any qubit state has two real parameters:</p><div class=\"eq\">|ψ⟩ = cos(θ/2)|0⟩ + e<sup>iφ</sup> sin(θ/2)|1⟩, 0 ≤ θ ≤ π, 0 ≤ φ &lt; 2π</div><p>It is drawn as the point (sin θ cos φ, sin θ sin φ, cos θ) on a unit sphere; these components are ⟨σ<sub>x</sub>⟩, ⟨σ<sub>y</sub>⟩, ⟨σ<sub>z</sub>⟩.</p><figure data-fig=\"m5-bloch\"></figure><ul><li><b>Poles:</b> north (θ = 0) is |0⟩ and south (θ = π) is |1⟩, the computational basis states, which give a definite measurement result.</li><li><b>Equator</b> (θ = π/2): equal superpositions, P(0) = P(1) = ½, differing only in relative phase φ: |+⟩ on +x (φ = 0), |−⟩ on −x (φ = π), |±i⟩ on ±y.</li><li>The z component cos θ = P(0) − P(1); orthogonal states are opposite points.</li></ul><p><b>(ii)</b> X = [0 1; 1 0] swaps the amplitudes: X(α|0⟩ + β|1⟩) = β|0⟩ + α|1⟩, so X|0⟩ = |1⟩, X|1⟩ = |0⟩ (bit flip). For the Bloch state, X|ψ⟩ = e<sup>iφ</sup>[sin(θ/2)|0⟩ + e<sup>−iφ</sup>cos(θ/2)|1⟩]; dropping the global phase, (θ, φ) → (π − θ, −φ), so</p><div class=\"eq\">(x, y, z) → (x, −y, −z): a rotation by π about the x axis</div><p>The poles are exchanged, |+⟩ is unchanged (on the axis) and |−⟩ only picks up a global phase −1.</p><figure data-fig=\"m5-gate-actions\"></figure>"
    }
