    {
     "n": "1",
     "answer": "<p>A <b>blackbody</b> is an ideal body that absorbs all radiation falling on it, at every frequency, and (in equilibrium) emits radiation whose spectrum depends only on its temperature. A practical model is a hollow cavity with a small hole: radiation entering the hole is reflected again and again inside and is almost completely absorbed, and the radiation leaving the hole is blackbody radiation.</p><figure data-fig=\"m1-blackbody\"></figure><p>For T<sub>1</sub> &lt; T<sub>2</sub> &lt; T<sub>3</sub>: each curve rises from zero, reaches a peak and falls to zero; the hotter curve lies entirely above the cooler one (total energy ∝ T<sup>4</sup>, Stefan–Boltzmann law) and its peak moves to higher frequency, shorter wavelength (Wien’s displacement law λ<sub>max</sub>T = b).</p><p><b>Failure of classical physics.</b> Rayleigh and Jeans treated the cavity radiation with classical equipartition (every mode has average energy k<sub>B</sub>T) and obtained</p><div class=\"eq\">u(ν) dν = (8πk<sub>B</sub>T/c<sup>3</sup>) ν<sup>2</sup> dν</div><p>This fits the measured spectrum at low frequencies but rises as ν<sup>2</sup> without limit, so the total energy ∫u dν would be infinite. Experiment shows the curve reaching a peak and falling to zero at high frequency. This failure in the ultraviolet is the <b>ultraviolet catastrophe</b>. Wien’s empirical law u = Aν<sup>3</sup>e<sup>−Bν/T</sup> fitted the high-frequency side but underestimated the infrared, so neither classical attempt worked everywhere.</p><figure data-fig=\"m1-uv-catastrophe\"></figure><p><b>Planck’s hypothesis (1900).</b></p><ul><li>Energy is exchanged between the wall oscillators and the radiation only in discrete quanta.</li><li>Each quantum has energy E = hν, so an oscillator can have only 0, hν, 2hν, 3hν, ….</li><li>The occupation of these levels follows Boltzmann statistics, probability ∝ e<sup>−E/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup>; the average energy per oscillator is then hν/(e<sup>hν/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup> − 1) instead of k<sub>B</sub>T.</li><li>High-frequency oscillators need a large quantum hν ≫ k<sub>B</sub>T, which is rarely available, so they carry almost no energy. This removes the catastrophe.</li></ul><p>Planck’s radiation law:</p><div class=\"eq\">u(ν) dν = (8πhν<sup>3</sup>/c<sup>3</sup>) · 1/(e<sup>hν/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup> − 1) dν</div><p><b>Low frequency, hν ≪ k<sub>B</sub>T.</b> Using e<sup>x</sup> ≈ 1 + x, e<sup>hν/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup> − 1 ≈ hν/k<sub>B</sub>T, so u(ν) dν ≈ (8πhν<sup>3</sup>/c<sup>3</sup>)(k<sub>B</sub>T/hν) dν = (8πν<sup>2</sup>k<sub>B</sub>T/c<sup>3</sup>) dν: the Rayleigh–Jeans law.</p><p><b>High frequency, hν ≫ k<sub>B</sub>T.</b> e<sup>hν/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup> − 1 ≈ e<sup>hν/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup>, so u(ν) dν ≈ (8πhν<sup>3</sup>/c<sup>3</sup>) e<sup>−hν/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup> dν: Wien’s law with A = 8πh/c<sup>3</sup> and B = h/k<sub>B</sub>, which falls to zero as ν → ∞.</p><p>So Planck’s single formula agrees with experiment at all frequencies and contains both earlier laws as limits.</p>"
    },
    {
     "n": "2",
     "answer": "<p><b>Set-up.</b> Silver is heated in an oven to give a beam of neutral silver atoms; the beam is collimated by narrow slits, passes between the poles of a magnet shaped to give a <b>non-uniform</b> field B<sub>z</sub>(z), and strikes a detector plate. Silver has one unpaired outer electron, so the atom’s magnetic moment is that of one electron spin.</p><figure data-fig=\"m1-stern-gerlach\"></figure><p><b>Principle.</b> A magnetic moment μ in a field has energy E = −μ·B. A uniform field only twists the moment; a field gradient exerts a force F<sub>z</sub> = μ<sub>z</sub> ∂B<sub>z</sub>/∂z, so atoms are deflected up or down according to μ<sub>z</sub>. For an electron μ = −g(e/2m)S with g ≈ 2.</p><p><b>Classical expectation.</b> Moments oriented at random would give all values of μ<sub>z</sub>, so a continuous smear on the plate.</p><p><b>Observation.</b> The beam splits into exactly two spots, one above and one below the centre line.</p><p><b>Evidence for spin quantisation.</b> The deflection is proportional to μ<sub>z</sub>, so two sharp spots mean μ<sub>z</sub> (and the angular momentum component) takes only two values. Orbital angular momentum l gives 2l + 1 spots, always an odd number, and silver’s ground state has l = 0, so the two spots cannot be orbital: they need a new angular momentum with 2s + 1 = 2, i.e. s = ½.</p><p><b>Conclusions.</b></p><ul><li>The electron has an intrinsic angular momentum, spin, not caused by any rotation of a charged ball.</li><li>The spin component along the field is quantised with only two values, S<sub>z</sub> = +ℏ/2 or −ℏ/2 (spin up and spin down).</li><li>Measuring along another axis (x) again gives only ±ℏ/2.</li><li>It is a real two-state quantum system, the basis of qubits, and it shows that measurement gives only certain allowed values (quantisation).</li></ul>"
    },
    {
     "n": "3a",
     "answer": "<p>The muon gains kinetic energy K = qV, so p = √(2mqV) and λ = h/p.</p><div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">K = qV = 1.6 × 10<sup>−19</sup> × 100 = 1.6 × 10<sup>−17</sup> J</div><div class=\"eq\">p = √(2 × 1.88 × 10<sup>−28</sup> × 1.6 × 10<sup>−17</sup>) = √(6.016 × 10<sup>−45</sup>) = 7.756 × 10<sup>−23</sup> kg m s<sup>−1</sup></div><div class=\"eq\">λ = 6.626 × 10<sup>−34</sup> / 7.756 × 10<sup>−23</sup> = 8.54 × 10<sup>−12</sup> m</div></div><div class=\"result\"><p>λ ≈ 8.54 × 10<sup>−12</sup> m = 8.54 pm (about 14 times shorter than an electron’s at 100 V, 0.123 nm, since the muon is ~207 times heavier)</p></div></div>"
    },
    {
     "n": "3b",
     "answer": "<p>Photon energy E = hν = hc/λ.</p><div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">E = 6.626 × 10<sup>−34</sup> × 3 × 10<sup>8</sup> / 589 × 10<sup>−9</sup> = 1.988 × 10<sup>−25</sup> / 5.89 × 10<sup>−7</sup></div><div class=\"eq\">= 3.375 × 10<sup>−19</sup> J</div><div class=\"eq\">In eV: 3.375 × 10<sup>−19</sup> / 1.602 × 10<sup>−19</sup> = 2.11 eV</div></div><div class=\"result\"><p>E ≈ 3.37 × 10<sup>−19</sup> J ≈ 2.11 eV</p></div></div>"
    },
    {
     "n": "4a",
     "answer": "<p>The bra is the conjugate transpose: ⟨ψ| = (1, −i), ⟨φ| = (2, 1).</p><div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">⟨ψ|φ⟩ = (1)(2) + (−i)(1) = 2 − i</div><div class=\"eq\">⟨φ|ψ⟩ = (2)(1) + (1)(i) = 2 + i = ⟨ψ|φ⟩*</div></div><div class=\"result\"><p>Inner product ⟨ψ|φ⟩ = 2 − i (and ⟨φ|ψ⟩ = 2 + i)</p></div></div><p>The outer product |ψ⟩⟨φ| has elements ψ<sub>i</sub>φ<sub>j</sub>*:</p><div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">|ψ⟩⟨φ| = (1, i)<sup>T</sup>(2, 1) = [1·2 1·1; i·2 i·1] = [2 1; 2i i]</div><div class=\"eq\">|φ⟩⟨ψ| = (2, 1)<sup>T</sup>(1, −i) = [2 −2i; 1 −i] = (|ψ⟩⟨φ|)<sup>†</sup></div></div><div class=\"result\"><p>|ψ⟩⟨φ| = [2 1; 2i i], a 2 × 2 operator</p></div></div>"
    },
    {
     "n": "4b",
     "answer": "<p>Put the vectors as columns and take the determinant; non-zero means independent.</p><div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">det [1 1 1; 0 1 1; 0 0 1] = 1 × 1 × 1 = 1 (upper triangular: product of the diagonal)</div><div class=\"eq\">Directly: c<sub>1</sub>v<sub>1</sub> + c<sub>2</sub>v<sub>2</sub> + c<sub>3</sub>v<sub>3</sub> = (c<sub>1</sub> + c<sub>2</sub> + c<sub>3</sub>, c<sub>2</sub> + c<sub>3</sub>, c<sub>3</sub>) = 0</div><div class=\"eq\">Third component: c<sub>3</sub> = 0; second: c<sub>2</sub> = 0; first: c<sub>1</sub> = 0</div></div><div class=\"result\"><p>det = 1 ≠ 0 and only the trivial combination gives zero, so v<sub>1</sub>, v<sub>2</sub>, v<sub>3</sub> are linearly independent and form a basis of ℝ<sup>3</sup>.</p></div></div>"
    },
    {
     "n": "5a",
     "answer": "<p>The tensor product V ⊗ W of a space V (dimension m, basis |e<sub>i</sub>⟩) and W (dimension n, basis |f<sub>j</sub>⟩) is the space spanned by the products |e<sub>i</sub>⟩ ⊗ |f<sub>j</sub>⟩, so its dimension is m·n. It describes composite systems such as two particles or two qubits.</p><p>Vectors multiply component by component: (a, b) ⊗ (c, d) = (ac, ad, bc, bd). Example: (1, 2) ⊗ (3, 4) = (3, 4, 6, 8).</p><figure data-fig=\"m2-tensor\"></figure><p><b>Importance in quantum mechanics.</b></p><ul><li>The state space of a composite system is the tensor product of the subsystem spaces: two spins, two qubits, a particle’s position and spin.</li><li>Dimensions multiply, so n qubits need 2<sup>n</sup> amplitudes; this is the source of quantum computing’s power.</li><li>States that are not a single product |a⟩ ⊗ |b⟩, such as the Bell state (|00⟩ + |11⟩)/√2, are entangled.</li><li>Operators on composite systems are built the same way, e.g. σ<sub>x</sub> ⊗ I acts on the first qubit only.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
     "n": "5b",
     "answer": "<p>Use (a, b) ⊗ (c, d) = (ac, ad, bc, bd).</p><div class=\"worked\"><div class=\"solve\"><div class=\"eq\">|00⟩ = (1, 0) ⊗ (1, 0) = (1·1, 1·0, 0·1, 0·0) = (1, 0, 0, 0)</div><div class=\"eq\">|01⟩ = (1, 0) ⊗ (0, 1) = (0, 1, 0, 0)</div><div class=\"eq\">|10⟩ = (0, 1) ⊗ (1, 0) = (0, 0, 1, 0)</div><div class=\"eq\">|11⟩ = (0, 1) ⊗ (0, 1) = (0, 0, 0, 1)</div></div><div class=\"result\"><p>|00⟩, |01⟩, |10⟩, |11⟩ are the four standard column vectors of ℂ<sup>4</sup>: an orthonormal basis of the two-qubit space (dimension 2 × 2 = 4).</p></div></div>"
    }
